#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148356} :activity/id #uuid "b830a80c-d680-54e0-801a-894c774ffea4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302 :topic/id #uuid "92b2c666-a4c0-5f99-83f5-ccf8e995ac64" :topic/math-academy-id 2471} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148684 :tutorial/content "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years.  The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of $t \\geq 0.$ For example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =120\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =120\\cdot {2}^{0} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed1b9eba-931a-5908-99c6-58b738afef7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1937 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "642a7826-566a-5e56-b810-cb6fb7d5fa0c" :step/math-academy-id 531824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147198 :question/id #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050886}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489253}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"} :step/id #uuid "c0ba8192-ba62-514c-b119-d4b80274d19c" :step/math-academy-id 531825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148683 :tutorial/content "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years? \n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t.\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t.$\n\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b,$ then use the formula $y = ab^t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65e48fd4-d6c3-5e02-aad1-9a8dae46b0bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1936 :tutorial/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"} :step/id #uuid "4ad684e1-3cee-5aab-8d3d-e3b084e25e37" :step/math-academy-id 531828 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147199 :question/id #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" :question/math-academy-id "e-1988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489252}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"} :step/id #uuid "c9b07cd7-55e0-5b22-8e5e-94373891336a" :step/math-academy-id 531829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147215 :question/id #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489179}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "5e381067-3801-5773-b9a5-d5c897aa20dc" :step/math-academy-id 531832}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147671}]}
